        "repository_id": 113,
        "snapshot_id": 173,
        "final_answer": "## Investigation Result\n\n**No, execution does not reach `retry_delay` when `submit_job` receives an invalid token.** The failure ordering is strict and sequential:\n\n### Entry Point — `submit_job` in `workflow.py` [E2_1]\n```python\ndef submit_job(token: str, attempt: int, transient: bool) -> dict:\n    verify_token(token)                    # ← line 8\n    delay = retry_delay(attempt, transient) # ← line 9\n    ...\n```\n\n### Token Helper — `verify_token` in `auth.py` [E4_1]\n```python\ndef verify_token(token: str) -> None:\n    if token != \"trusted\":\n        raise PermissionError(\"Invalid token\")\n```\n\n### Failure Ordering Explanation\n\n1. **`verify_token(token)` executes first** (line 8 of `workflow.py`). If the token is anything other than the literal `\"trusted\"`, it raises a `PermissionError` immediately [E4_1]. This exception propagates out of `submit_job` and aborts the function.\n\n2. **`retry_delay(attempt, transient)` on line 9 is never reached** because the `PermissionError` from step 1 causes early exit. There is no try/except or guard around the `verify_token` call that would allow execution to continue to the retry logic.\n\n3. Only if `verify_token` succeeds (i.e., `token == \"trusted\"`) does execution proceed to `retry_delay` in `policy.py` [E6_1], which then evaluates the attempt/transient parameters and returns either a computed delay or `None`.\n\nIn summary: **token validation always precedes retry-delay evaluation**. An invalid token causes a `PermissionError` before any retry logic can be consulted.",
