        "final_answer": "Here is the investigation result:\n\n### Files Examined\n1. **README.md** (public retry guide) — [E2_1]\n2. **workflow.py** (`submit_job` entry point) — [E3_1]\n3. **policy.py** (actual retry policy) — [E4_1]\n\n---\n\n### The Mismatch\n\n| Source | Claim / Behavior |\n|---|---|\n| **README.md** [E2_1] | *\"The public retry guide claims transient jobs wait at most **60 seconds**.\"* |\n| **policy.py** [E4_1] | `retry_delay(attempt, transient)` returns `min(2 ** attempt, 300)`. At **attempt 10**, `2^10 = 1024`, capped at **300**. |\n| **workflow.py** [E3_1] | `submit_job` delegates to `retry_delay`; at attempt 10 it will return `{\"status\": \"scheduled\", \"delay\": 300}`. |\n\nAt transient attempt 10, the policy returns a **300-second** delay, not the **60-second** maximum advertised in the documentation.\n\n### Root Cause\n\nThe policy caps the exponential backoff at **300 seconds** (`min(2 ** attempt, 300)`), but the public-facing guide states the cap is **60 seconds**. These two values are inconsistent. There is no code path that enforces a 60-second ceiling; the only hard limit is 300.\n\n### Proposed Resolution (Without Modifying Code)\n\nSince we cannot modify code, the resolution must be documentary or operational:\n\n1. **Align the documentation with the actual policy.** Update README.md [E2_1] to accurately state that the maximum transient retry delay is **300 seconds**, not 60. This is the simplest fix—correct the claim rather than the implementation.\n\n2. **Alternatively, if 60 seconds is the intended business requirement**, add an explicit note in README.md flagging the discrepancy as a known bug and directing users to file an issue against `policy.py` [E4_1]. This preserves honesty while signaling that the current behavior does not match the promise.\n\nIn either case, the root inconsistency is between the stated maximum of 60s in the guide and the hardcoded 300s cap in the policy function.",
